### Database Schema (SQLite)

The tool uses a relational database structure to maintain the link between physical files and their historical snapshots.

#### 1. `files` Table

This table tracks the files you have chosen to archive.
| Column | Type | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `id` | INTEGER | Primary Key (Unique File ID). |
| `rel_path` | TEXT | The relative path of the file from the project root (Unique). |

#### 2. `versions` Table

This table stores every snapshot/version of the tracked files.
| Column | Type | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `id` | INTEGER | Primary Key (Unique Version ID). |
| `file_id` | INTEGER | Foreign Key referencing `files.id`. |
| `content` | TEXT | The actual source code/text content of the file. |
| `note` | TEXT | A custom note or "Auto-save" tag for the version. |
| `stamp` | TIMESTAMP| Automatic timestamp of when the version was saved. |

---

### Key Technical Insights:

* **Integrity:** If a file is moved or renamed, the tool will treat the new name as a new file, but the old versions remain safely stored under the original relative path in the database.
* **Storage:** Using `TEXT` for content storage in SQLite is efficient for source code, as it handles encoding and large strings natively.
* **Queries:** History is fetched using an `ASC` (Ascending) sort on `stamp` to ensure the logic of versioning (1, 2, 3...) remains chronologically correct.

